1
00:03:24,705 --> 00:03:30,705
He was never in time for his classes... He wasn't in time for his
dinner... Then one day... he wasn't in his time at all.

2
00:06:49,411 --> 00:06:55,411
Back to the Future is a adventure, comedy, science
fiction film directed by Robert Zemeckis.

3
00:10:14,117 --> 00:10:20,117
Eighties teenager Marty McFly is accidentally sent back in time to 1955, inadvertently
disrupting his parents' first meeting and attracting his mother's romantic interest.

4
00:13:38,823 --> 00:13:44,823
Marty must repair the damage to history by rekindling his parents' romance and - with
the help of his eccentric inventor friend Doc Brown - return to 1985.

5
00:17:03,529 --> 00:17:09,529
Marty McFly, played by Michael J. Fox.

6
00:20:28,235 --> 00:20:34,235
Emmett Brown, played by Christopher Lloyd.

7
00:23:52,941 --> 00:23:58,941
George McFly, played by Crispin Glover.

8
00:27:17,647 --> 00:27:23,647
Lorraine Baines, played by Lea Thompson.

9
00:30:42,352 --> 00:30:48,352
Jennifer Parker, played by Claudia Wells.

10
00:34:07,058 --> 00:34:13,058
Biff Tannen, played by Thomas F. Wilson.

11
00:37:31,764 --> 00:37:37,764
Back to the Future is a 1985 American science fiction film directed by
Robert Zemeckis and written by Zemeckis and Bob Gale.

12
00:40:56,470 --> 00:41:02,470
It stars Michael J.

13
00:44:21,176 --> 00:44:27,176
Fox, Christopher Lloyd, Lea Thompson, Crispin Glover, and Thomas F.

14
00:47:45,882 --> 00:47:51,882
Set in 1985, it follows Marty McFly (Fox), a teenager accidentally sent back to 1955 in a time-traveling DeLorean automobile built by his eccentric scientist friend Emmett "Doc" Brown (Lloyd),
where he inadvertently prevents his future parents from falling in love – threatening his own existence – and is forced to reconcile them and somehow get back to the future.

15
00:51:10,588 --> 00:51:16,588
Gale and Zemeckis conceived the idea for Back to the Future in 1980.

16
00:54:35,294 --> 00:54:41,294
They were desperate for a successful film after numerous collaborative failures, but the project was rejected more than forty times
by various studios because it was not considered raunchy enough to compete with the successful comedies of the era.

17
00:58:00,000 --> 00:58:06,000
A development deal was secured with Universal Pictures following
Zemeckis's success directing Romancing the Stone (1984).

18
01:01:24,705 --> 01:01:30,705
Fox was the first choice to portray Marty but was
unavailable; Eric Stoltz was cast instead.

19
01:04:49,411 --> 01:04:55,411
Shortly after principal photography began in November 1984, Zemeckis determined Stoltz was not right for the part and made the
concessions necessary to hire Fox, including re-filming scenes already shot with Stoltz and adding $4 million to the budget.

20
01:08:14,117 --> 01:08:20,117
Back to the Future was filmed in and around California and on sets at
Universal Studios, and concluded the following April.

21
01:11:38,823 --> 01:11:44,823
After highly successful test screenings, the release date was brought forward to July 3, 1985,
giving the film more time in theaters during the busiest period of the theatrical year.

22
01:15:03,529 --> 01:15:09,529
The change resulted in a rushed post-production
schedule and some incomplete special effects.

23
01:18:28,235 --> 01:18:34,235
Nevertheless, Back to the Future was a critical and commercial success, earning
$398 million to become the highest-grossing film of 1985 worldwide.

24
01:21:52,941 --> 01:21:58,941
Critics praised the story, humor, and the cast,
particularly Fox, Lloyd, Thompson, and Glover.

25
01:25:17,647 --> 01:25:23,647
It received multiple award nominations and won an Academy
Award, three Saturn Awards, and a Hugo Award.

26
01:28:42,352 --> 01:28:48,352
Its theme song, "The Power of Love" by Huey Lewis
and the News, was also a success.

27
01:32:07,058 --> 01:32:13,058
Back to the Future has since grown in esteem and is now considered by critics and audiences to
be one of the greatest science fiction films and among the best films ever made.

28
01:35:31,764 --> 01:35:37,764
In 2007, the United States Library of Congress selected it for
preservation in the National Film Registry.

29
01:38:56,470 --> 01:39:02,470
The film was followed by two sequels, Back to the Future Part II
(1989) and Back to the Future Part III (1990).

30
01:42:21,176 --> 01:42:27,176
Spurred by the film's dedicated fan following and effect on popular culture, Universal Studios launched a multimedia
franchise, which now includes video games, theme park rides, an animated television series, and a stage musical.

31
01:45:45,882 --> 01:45:51,882
Its enduring popularity has prompted numerous books about its
production, documentaries, and commercials.

32
01:49:10,588 --> 01:49:16,588
The film explores themes of flying car, race against
time, clock tower, car race, lightning.

33
01:52:35,294 --> 01:52:41,294
The tone is lighthearted and thought_provoking.
